#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495189} :activity/id #uuid "45da9bbc-1512-5651-8f30-bc8c978f6a42" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495178 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1673e8-dffa-58ad-a21d-370ac4f21d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494409} :answer-field/id #uuid "e18cc19c-c2dd-5d83-85f6-24d5473b0766" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64d62ce0-8437-5a77-8100-0fa04bf9d1d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343690" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-2)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.8{y}^{″}=-ky+(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-2).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{4}y=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=20\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "04593abc-de35-5ea9-a146-5ea660c1a706" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495189 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3210051c-3999-5418-903e-b15e38e02322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333450} {:answer/id #uuid "38b5b020-4f8f-567f-a7cc-df5b75d4aa8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333513} {:answer/id #uuid "64c4f9a2-40a9-51cb-89ef-5dd5f4612358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333903} {:answer/id #uuid "93fa9c39-59bb-537d-b57b-efe61e7a1d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334306} {:answer/id #uuid "bb46ab48-8ea8-59c5-8936-9b90e3e9cf81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334306} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47f96a3-e6a8-5ed6-8d03-4b9a5051d156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3cf0fed1-cc47-5050-81b5-0e3536d5ca7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64647" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "2265a2b8-a125-50ed-bfc1-8d6afde6c2a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b13814-4c69-5bb6-8b57-73e807680e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354754} {:answer/id #uuid "5099e87a-dff9-5c35-b894-e19a23671e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355384} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed0d800-100d-5331-be07-cf9b7d1f922b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355645} {:answer/id #uuid "7cddcd84-f830-5b88-a16f-f75d5f927c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355763} {:answer/id #uuid "bff3e8db-ba05-59e7-ae17-59543c35e946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355763} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d8a8347-f78f-5143-a87c-1d1b551dcbcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2cead4d-0d70-5529-b159-16c77413f78b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94238" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=6\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{6}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{6}t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\sqrt{6}.$"} :step/id #uuid "3fe268b2-a12f-5127-8880-a5efc009faa7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495206}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d4de519-7dc3-5a63-91dc-88fb097de927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315559} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e5b3fd-f8d6-55a8-9a84-9bc4da788fc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9c255544-1d5d-5734-8a75-e3f42b3984e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343679" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.8{y}^{″}=-ky+2cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{4}y=\\frac{5}{2}cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\frac{5}{2}cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{5}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}=5\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "683c98ce-5d82-50d3-a3f8-d24ea690b317" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495178}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ac45f2-8682-5e38-90a5-af6b3b0df052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442734} {:answer/id #uuid "720de89b-25be-516b-9df5-32bca90894e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442735} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3b3ddc-f2b0-5960-a120-6e3182615388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442736} {:answer/id #uuid "90774d44-8df7-5a01-b829-eff4553b5ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442737} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5401b3-3249-566f-8e5f-01020ae0590b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442736} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3260424-876c-50ae-ac8b-7b96731c9907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee642054-1d27-50a4-8f05-345ca578dfc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343743" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 9D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solution II matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "c95a38fe-1860-539e-b737-8be81b3f72b1"}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494304}]}
